<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest. Every app uploaded to App Store Connect needs one, and the
  aggregate of this file and every bundled framework's own is what Apple
  checks. Confirm the aggregate with Product > Archive > Generate Privacy
  Report before an upload rather than trusting this file alone.

  All four answers are the empty one, and each is a claim about the binary:

  - NSPrivacyTracking false, NSPrivacyTrackingDomains empty: the app contacts
    nothing. package.mjs fails the build on any asset fetched over http(s),
    and the iOS build replaces ScoreClient().auto(...) with an unconnected
    client, so there is no network code left to track anybody with.
  - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes empty: nothing is collected. Scores, codex
    progress and settings stay in localStorage on the device; Game Center
    scores go to Apple under the player's own account, which is Apple's
    collection and not ours to declare.
  - NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes empty. The one most guides get wrong here is
    NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults, which is required when *your*
    binary or a bundled SDK calls NSUserDefaults. localStorage in a WKWebView
    is WebKit's own store and WebKit is a system framework; @capacitor/ios
    contains no UserDefaults call and ships its own manifest declaring none.
    Adding @capacitor/preferences, or anything that writes UserDefaults from
    Swift, changes that -- and so does adding file timestamp, disk space or
    system boot time APIs.

  magmacrunch.com/privacy/george-boole/ is the human-readable version, and the
  two must stay in step.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
